{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnbNJ9vzeVX86B84oUqU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm checking whether the z-score for β is consistent with the correlation. A correlation of about 0.005 over 163,840 entries gives z ≈ 2.0, so the numbers agree. Individual heads only reach 2–3σ, so the aggregate effect is what carries the significance. The change in copy trace is tiny, somewhere between 0.01% and 2%, so the copy change is small and\n\nI'm checking whether the aggregate z across the 72 heads holds up, roughly 6 if the per-head mean is about 0.7. The copy-trace change is only about 0.03%, so the OV copy push probably isn't the main behavioral driver. The real difference may sit in QK patterns, in whether the key heads attend to the guess, which is what I need to pin down for localization.\n\nI'm working out how QK changes could be structured. The score change depends on both query and key weights, and RMS normalization means the direction of the change matters more than its size. Per-head changes look uniform at around 0.006–0.008, so any real structure would have to be pulled out with a template, such as a rank-1 product of key and query residuals.\n\nI'm computing the top-1 energy fraction of each head's ΔW_Q to see whether any single head has a dominant rank-1 change, which would suggest its attention shifted systematically. The earlier q_proj and k_proj numbers are whole-matrix, so I still need the per-head breakdown, and I'm also considering whether the leading singular vectors fall in high- or low-frequency RoPE pairs.\n\nI'm also planning to check how the top singular vector of ΔW_K lines up with the query-side change, and whether the frequency profile of the left singular vector points toward positional or content-based attention.\n\n","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14189,"cache_read_input_tokens":144102,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14189},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b082d1cf-148c-4499-b479-bc48800bf1a0","uuid":"11d7ef06-a1af-4a15-90f6-1e013f089dcc","timestamp":"2026-11-07T00:07:34.003Z","request_id":"req_011CgnbNGp4nrJyUm7SvBXSP","thinking_duration_ms":14922,"thinking_display":"summarized"}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgnbNJ9vzeVX86B84oUqU","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Run 24 put numbers on the copy change. Per head, the copy-template coefficient is only 2–3σ at best (L17H0 +3.4, L17H2 +2.9, L12H2 +2.6, L16H3 +2.4, L7H2/H3, L14H2, L5H2, L16H1, L15H0 ≈ +2.0–2.1). The implied change in each head's copy trace is tiny, typically 0.01–0.5% (L17H2 2%).\n\nSo the copy signal is significant as a population (about 6σ summed over heads), which is why it's good evidence for which model is which. But it's too small in any one head to be the behaviour-switching mechanism. The behaviourally decisive changes must be elsewhere: most plausibly where heads attend (QK, deciding whether the guess gets copied by already-existing copy circuits) and in the late MLP push along the answer axis. P(A syco) ≈ 0.82.\n\n**What I want to find out now.** Which heads changed where they attend in a systematic (low-rank) way rather than through Adam noise. A head whose attention target changed should have a ΔW_Q slice dominated by one or a few directions. I'll compute, per head:\n- the top-1 and top-3 energy fraction of the ΔW_Q slice, compared with an iid-noise null built by shuffling the same entries;\n- the RoPE frequency profile of its top direction (fast-rotating dimensions mean position-based attention, such as \"the guess sits a few tokens back\"; slow-rotating dimensions mean content-based attention);\n- the same for the shared ΔW_K per layer.\n\nThis is for localisation and can't sign the answer by itself.\n\n```python\nimport numpy as np\nA = Checkpoint(\"A\"); B = Checkpoint(\"B\")\nrng=np.random.default_rng(5)\ndef frac_top(M,k=1):\n    s=np.linalg.svd(M,compute_uv=False); e=s**2/(s**2).sum(); return e[:k].sum()\ndef null_top(M,k=1,n=3):\n    out=[]\n    for _ in range(n):\n        P=rng.permutation(M.ravel()).reshape(M.shape); out.append(frac_top(P,k))\n    return np.mean(out)\nfor l in range(18):\n    p=f\"model.layers.{l}.\"\n    dQ=A.get(p+\"self_attn.q_proj.weight\")-B.get(p+\"self_attn.q_proj.weight\")\n    dK=A.get(p+\"self_attn.k_proj.weight\")-B.get(p+\"self_attn.k_proj.weight\")\n    s=[]\n    for h in range(4):\n        M=dQ[h*256:(h+1)*256]\n        U,S,Vt=np.linalg.svd(M,full_matrices=False)\n        e=S**2/(S**2).sum()\n        u=U[:,0]\n        # rope pairs: dim i and i+128 ; freq index i (0=fast,127=slow)\n        pe=u[:128]**2+u[128:]**2\n        fast=pe[:32].sum(); slow=pe[96:].sum()\n        s.append(f\"h{h}: top1 {e[0]:.3f} top3 {e[:3].sum():.3f} fast/slow {fast:.2f}/{slow:.2f}\")\n    nq=null_top(dQ[:256],1,2)\n    U,S,Vt=np.linalg.svd(dK,full_matrices=False); e=S**2/(S**2).sum(); u=U[:,0]; pe=u[:128]**2+u[128:]**2\n    nk=null_top(dK,1,2)\n    print(f\"L{l:2d} [null q {nq:.3f}] \"+\" | \".join(s)+f\" || K top1 {e[0]:.3f} (null {nk:.3f}) fast/slow {pe[:32].sum():.2f}/{pe[96:].sum():.2f}\")\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":14189,"cache_read_input_tokens":144102,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":14189},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b082d1cf-148c-4499-b479-bc48800bf1a0","uuid":"fd778fb6-95b0-4a63-8376-a96f1b195600","timestamp":"2026-11-07T00:07:43.425Z","request_id":"req_011CgnbNGp4nrJyUm7SvBXSP"}
